## 3. Commercial and Noncommercial Purposes

A "Commercial Purpose" is a purpose that is primarily intended for or directed
toward commercial advantage or monetary compensation **through the software
itself**. The following are Commercial Purposes:

(a) selling, renting, leasing, licensing, or otherwise charging anyone for the
software or a derivative work, or for access to it;

(b) operating the software or a derivative work as a hosted or network service
for which you charge, including software-as-a-service, paid hosting, or paid
access;

(c) charging for support, consulting, customization, or maintenance that is
substantially based on the software;

(d) including the software or a derivative work in a product, bundle, or service
for which you charge, or using it as a paid feature of a product or service;

(e) using the software or a derivative work as the primary basis of a business
whose revenue comes from the software.

The following are **not** Commercial Purposes, even if you are a for-profit
entity or receive incidental revenue:

(f) internal use, evaluation, development, testing, prototyping, continuous
integration, or running the software on your own machines or infrastructure,
without charging anyone for the software;

(g) personal, hobby, educational, research, or portfolio use;

(h) a personal or hobby project that carries incidental advertising or
donations, where the software is not itself the product being sold;

(i) using the software on a website that markets or promotes your own unrelated
products or services.

Charities, nonprofits, schools, and government bodies are subject to the same
rules as everyone else; they receive no special exemption under this license.

## 4. Distribution, Copyleft, and Source Code

You may distribute the software or derivative works only if you:

(a) license the entire work, including all modifications, under this same
